  "worked_solution": "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({e}^{x}))=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({e}^{x}))={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\sqrt{1-y})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+1))=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({y}^{2}+1))=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({y}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
